rent reinforcement learning for  those that aren't familiar  uh with it is a bit different than  standard you know supervised or  unsupervised  learning where you have a data set and  you're training off of this  this static data set in reinforcement  learning you have an agent that's  interacting with an environment in this  came  in this case an atari game and so the  data set itself is is what the agent  collects as it interacts with the  environment and so it's a little  the dynamics are a bit different and so  this is what the runners intended to do  to basically  ask the agent what action do you want me  to perform now execute it in the  environment and then log  things and checkpoint uh your parameters  so you can restore  things and but what i want to focus on  in this talk is  this these components over here so we  have our agent which is  basically what's making the decisions of  what what joystick movements to make  what actions to take  and it makes use of something we call a  replay memory which you can think of  as the data set so this is a just  basically a memory buffer  where the agent is storing all the  experiences that that it has received  from the environment and then it can  sample batches from that to perform its  learning  um and so when we originally wrote  dopamine it was in tensorflow tensorflow  version one um  and so i i what i want to do now is kind  of go through some of the pain points  that we went through in developing  dopamine  um specifically with with the version of  tensorflow that we were using at a time  so when you see this uh  this red bubble um those are the pain  points that i'm trying to highlight  okay so um i'll start with the dqn agent  which was  this uh agent that was introduced by  deepmind in 2015.  um so we have our class definition there  and when one of the difficulties that we  had  or that wasn't as easy as we would have  liked to do is in building the replay  buffer  so uh this is uh the code that's  actual